2490234375, -0.0099945068359375, 0.0010223388671875, -0.0089569091796875, 0.0197601318359375, 0.017913818359375, 0.0159912109375, -0.05072021484375, -0.0015659332275390625, -0.032989501953125, 0.0035381317138671875, 0.00885009765625, 0.0115203857421875, 0.008087158203125, -0.0232086181640625, 0.0099639892578125, -0.003376007080078125, -0.0194549560546875, -0.0008416175842285156, -0.0194244384765625, -0.053070068359375, 0.005970001220703125, 0.018707275390625, 0.0006227493286132812, 0.00737762451171875, 0.01123809814453125, -0.018829345703125, 0.0091400146484375, -0.023712158203125, -0.01299285888671875, -0.033233642578125, 0.00994873046875, 0.0113067626953125, -0.04278564453125, -0.0006451606750488281, 0.017059326171875, -0.01715087890625, 0.0254974365234375, 0.01045989990234375, 0.000545501708984375, 0.0238494873046875, 0.01155853271484375, 0.0306243896484375, -0.00508880615234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is ubiquitous in oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains challenging, as existing methods often focus on global synchronization or amplitude-based measures and may miss subtle spatial organization. To address this gap, we ask: How can we robustly and efficiently characterize the evolving spatial organization of phases in oscillatory systems, including during nonstationary or partially synchronized regimes?\n\nHere, we introduce a framework based on spatial ordinal patterns to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of oscillatory systems. In this approach, we focus on the phase of oscillators rather than amplitude, as phase relationships more directly capture synchronization and wave propagation phenomena that are central to many applications, such as neural dynamics. Specifically, for each oscillator and its spatial neighbors, we construct a symbolic pattern by ranking the phases; for example, a triplet of oscillators with phases ordered as (low, medium, high) is assigned a distinct symbol. To handle cases where neighboring phases are nearly equal\u2014common in synchronized clusters\u2014we introduce additional symbols representing ties, ensuring that the symbolic encoding faithfully represents both gradients and plateaus in phase.\n\nThis symbolic representation encodes local spatial ordering relations, capturing both phase gradients and synchronized clusters within a single framework. From these patterns, we define a spatial permutation entropy by computing the distribution of observed symbols across the network at each time point and measuring their diversity. High entropy indicates a rich variety of spatial organizations, while low entropy reflects uniformity or strong clustering. This metric enables the detection of transient dynamics and regime transitions as they occur.\n\nWe demonstrate that our approach distinguishes phase-locked states with identical levels of global synchronization but distinct spatial organization, as well as characterizes partially synchronized states. For example, in synthetic oscillator networks, we show that spatial permutation entropy differentiates between traveling waves, chimera states, and globally synchronized regimes, even when traditional order parameters are similar. We further apply the method to resting-state EEG recordings from human volunteers, where it distinguishes different behavioral or cognitive conditions (such as eyes open versus eyes closed) within individual volunteers. Compared to conventional amplitude-based or global phase metrics, our approach provides finer discrimination of spatial phase organization and transient changes.\n\nIn summary, our framework offers a novel, phase-based symbolic analysis that advances the field by enabling robust, interpretable quantification of spatiotemporal patterns in oscillatory systems, with demonstrated utility in both synthetic and real-world neural data."}}